# Custom Hooks Best Practices

## Purpose and Scope

This rule enforces comprehensive standards for React custom hooks architecture, patterns, and implementation. It ensures consistent hook design, proper TypeScript integration, error handling, performance optimization, and maintainable code structure across the application. The rule establishes clear naming conventions, file organization, and development patterns for all custom hooks.

## Standards and Requirements

### Hook Architecture Standards

#### Naming Conventions - STRICTLY ENFORCE
```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Consistent naming patterns
export function useBeers() { ... }           // Collection hook
export function useBeer(id: string) { ... }  // Single item hook
export function useFilteredBeers(filter: FilterFn) { ... } // Filtered collection

export function useFood() { ... }            // Collection hook
export function useFoodItem(slug: string) { ... } // Single item hook
export function useFilteredFood(filter: FilterFn) { ... } // Filtered collection

// ✅ CORRECT - Utility hooks
export function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, defaultValue: T) { ... }
export function useSessionStorage<T>(key: string, defaultValue: T) { ... }
export function useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay: number) { ... }

// ❌ WRONG - Inconsistent naming
export function getBeerData() { ... }        // Missing 'use' prefix
export function useBeersList() { ... }       // Inconsistent with collection pattern
export function useGetBeer() { ... }         // Redundant 'get'
```

